candidates.signatureMoves:
1. Free detection, paid fixing. A plain-Node sweep covers all 124 repos in about 15 s at zero tokens (measured 14.5 s for the git checks). Tokens are spent only on findings that will become cards, and only in the roughly 7 repos with a session in the last 14 days. Dormant repos never get an agent.
2. A canary repo with 5 planted faults is reset and swept every night. The header reads "canary 5/5" or turns red and labels every clean verdict unverified. This makes L1 part of the structure instead of a habit.
3. Sealed by construction, not by prompt:
   - git worktrees carry no .env;
   - dontAsk plus an allowlist denies everything else;
   - a pre-push git hook always exits 1;
   - foreign clones, inbox-reading job logs, fetched page bodies and advisory prose never enter a brief.
   A night run therefore never holds untrusted content alongside an outbound channel. The only outbound step is Wes's click, run by deterministic code.
4. Learned test commands. The night only runs the test command a daytime session already ran green in that repo in the last 30 days, mined from transcript tool calls by cwd. 124 repos need no per-repo config file, and night runs never invent a test command.
5. A noise floor that tunes itself and leaves no guilt pile:
   - one daily line, at the end of the first reply only;
   - Discard reasons feed candidates.jsonl;
   - a chore under 35% merged after 8 cards drops to zero-token Noticed;
   - cards expire after 3 nights, because detection is free and a real problem comes back.
   Day sessions can also tag finishable work night:ok in wrap, and the night picks it up.
